\section*{Many primes with power-smooth predecessors}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 011; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that infinitely many totient values have more than $n^{1-\varepsilon}$ preimages for every positive $\varepsilon$, using many primes whose predecessors are very smooth.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Write $P^+(m)$ for the largest prime factor of a positive integer. For every fixed real $0<\delta<1/4$, the manuscript claims
\[
 |\{p\text{ prime}:2x<p\le5x,\ P^+(p-1)\le x^{\delta}\}|
 \ge x^{1-o(1)}\qquad(x\to\infty).
\]
Equivalently, for each fixed $\delta$ in this range and each $\eta>0$, the count is at least $x^{1-\eta}$ for all sufficiently large real $x$; the threshold can depend on both parameters. The smoothness bound applies to the full shifted integer $p-1$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 011; 2026-09-24), weighted dilation graphs are transferred to independent residue models, with signed-memory and frequency estimates controlling the error. The resulting supply of smooth prime predecessors yields many distinct prime products but comparatively few totient values.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 011; 2026-09-24).

